      "where": "Bibliography"
    }
  ],
  "technical_issues": [
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "A sun-synchronous orbit provides a consistent local solar time, but does not keep the spacecraft continuously illuminated. The claim that the descending path is constantly in sunlight is unsupported because the local time of the ascending or descending node is not specified."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The report says RAAN indicates the launch-facility location. RAAN describes the inertial orientation of the orbital plane; achieving the desired plane requires launch timing and injection geometry. The stated elements also lack a clear epoch."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "No sensor, spatial or spectral resolution, swath, or revisit analysis demonstrates that a 600 km satellite can produce useful fluid-lensing reef images or cover the proposed areas at the stated cadence."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "Mount Pleasant is identified as a radio observatory, but the report does not establish spacecraft TT&C/data-link capability, compatible frequencies, antenna availability, or operational support there."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "The drag sail is proposed without sizing, a predicted decay/re-entry time, or evidence that the disposal plan meets an applicable debris-mitigation timeline."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Design",
      "issue": "The report presents STK outputs but does not include the requested relevant STK project files in the submitted material."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "A coherent preliminary Australian reef-monitoring concept with useful STK access analysis and broad coverage of required mission sections. Marks are limited by unverified payload feasibility, incomplete operational and disposal justification, a few orbital-geometry misconceptions, and presentation and referencing weaknesses."
}
